  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 415 AM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low over northwestern Ontario,\nwith frontal boundary dropping through the upper Midwest into\nthe central plains. High stretches from Alberta and western\nSaskatchewan through central & eastern Montana into the far \nwestern Dakotas. Upper level analysis places low over far \nnortheast Manitoba, with trough dipping south-southwestward into\nthe eastern Dakotas. Modest ridging noted over the Rockies \nahead of a low spinning offshore from the Pacific Northwest. \nOver our area, some very light rain showers continue to straddle\nthe North Dakota and South Dakota border, but have been mainly \nhanging a bit to the south. \n\nFor today, low chances for rain showers will continue through\nthe morning along the NoDak/SoDak border, diminishing by mid-\nday. Northwest winds will remain a bit breezy over central North\nDakota in the wake of yesterday's frontal passage, while over\nthe west winds will be lighter as surface high pushes in.\nHigh temperatures will also be notably cooler, running about 10\nto 15 degrees below average. Winds then go light overnight with\nskies clearing over the north. This will lead to some cold\ntemperatures with guidance suggesting mid 30s possible over\nnorthwest North Dakota. Will have to take a close look at this today\nto see if any frost headlines may be needed.\n\nSunday starts out quiet with upper ridging developing ahead of\nthe Pacific upper low which is expected to move onshore by\nmorning, sliding towards the northern Rockies through the day.\nIn response, a lee side surface low develops with increasing\nsoutheasterly winds in the afternoon, primarily over southwest\nNorth Dakota.\n\nSunday night into early Monday, upper low moves quickly over \nthe northern Rockies into the plains, but some question does \nremain on whether at this point it will take a bit more \nnorthern track through the southern Prairie Provinces, or hang \nmore towards the international border, which would also be \nreflected in the potential path for the surface low lifting \nnortheastward. Either way brings elevated precipitation chances\nto our area, but a more northern path would result in lower\nprecipitation accumulations. Right now NBM guidance is holding \na 50 percent or greater chance for seeing a half inch or more of\nprecipitation along and east of a line from Bismarck to Minot, \nbut is now lower for western areas than what we saw yesterday. \nPrecipitation pushes north/east exiting the area Monday \nafternoon/early evening with the potential for a little to wrap \nback in over far northwestern areas a bit later, depending on \nthe aforementioned track. \n\nAs the surface low lifts northeast, winds will shift westerly\nover our area through the day on Monday, strongest over western\nNorth Dakota where ECMWF EFI focuses a broad area of 0.7 to \n0.8, in line with going forecast of potential wind gusts of \naround 50-55 mph, though will have to watch this trend closely \nto see if there is an upward shift at all as there is the \npotential of some stronger winds mixing down.\n \nQuieter weather expected in the wake of the system for Tuesday,\nthough temperatures do remain on the cool side. For the\nremainder of the work week latest NBM guidance does show some\nspread in high temperature forecasts, but overall trend does\nappear to be upward. Precipitation forecast remains a bit tricky\nas some questions remain on the path of a potential upper trough\nas it approaches the plains.",
